{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-339746" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y(1),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny(1)≈{{field-1}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9fce07b29e9bb957d3bba052038b804"} {"question" "q-339746" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=1,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x-y+(2{x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)-\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0+1=1.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{3}(2x+2{x}_{\\text{new}}+y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(1)≈1.00.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9fce07b29e9bb957d3bba052038b804"} {"question" "q-339746" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9446c68c60a0534a10a72f849a8cbe2e143d02ce1b73358e1b29ed25bed1591d"} {"question" "q-339746" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9fce07b29e9bb957d3bba052038b804"} {"question" "q-339746" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.03"] ["math" "1.00"] ["math" "1.21"] ["math" "2.31"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9fce07b29e9bb957d3bba052038b804"} {"question" "q-339746" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.00"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9fce07b29e9bb957d3bba052038b804"} {"question" "q-339755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x+2y,\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with one step to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{3}),$ rounded to two decimal places\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{3})≈{{field-1}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9fce07b29e9bb957d3bba052038b804"} {"question" "q-339755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=\\frac{1}{3},$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1}{3}-0=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3x+2y+(3{x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+2y+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+2y+3{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{6}(3x+2y+3{x}_{\\text{new}})+\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+8y)+\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{6}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+8y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+8y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=0+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Hence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(3x+3{x}_{\\text{new}}+8y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(3\\cdot 0+3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}+8\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\,(\\frac{1}{3})≈0.25.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9fce07b29e9bb957d3bba052038b804"} {"question" "q-339755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9446c68c60a0534a10a72f849a8cbe2e143d02ce1b73358e1b29ed25bed1591d"} {"question" "q-339755" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9fce07b29e9bb957d3bba052038b804"} {"question" "q-339755" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.25"] ["math" "0.43"] ["math" "1.25"] ["math" "1.42"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9fce07b29e9bb957d3bba052038b804"} {"question" "q-339755" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.25"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9fce07b29e9bb957d3bba052038b804"} {"question" "q-339865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x+2y,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈{{field-1}},\\,y(1)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9fce07b29e9bb957d3bba052038b804"} {"question" "q-339865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((x+2y)+({x}_{\\text{new}}+2{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+2y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+2y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+\\frac{1}{2}+6\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{4} \\\\  & =3.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=3.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+6y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}+1+6\\cdot (3.25)) \\\\  & =10.50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈3.25,\\,y(1)≈10.50.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9fce07b29e9bb957d3bba052038b804"} {"question" "q-339865" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9446c68c60a0534a10a72f849a8cbe2e143d02ce1b73358e1b29ed25bed1591d"} {"question" "q-339865" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9fce07b29e9bb957d3bba052038b804"} {"question" "q-339865" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3.50"] ["math" "2.89"] ["math" "3.25"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9fce07b29e9bb957d3bba052038b804"} {"question" "q-339865" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3.25"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9fce07b29e9bb957d3bba052038b804"} {"question" "q-339865" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9fce07b29e9bb957d3bba052038b804"} {"question" "q-339865" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-10.59"] ["math" "10.50"] ["math" "10.93"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9fce07b29e9bb957d3bba052038b804"} {"question" "q-339865" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10.50"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9fce07b29e9bb957d3bba052038b804"} {"question" "q-339867" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=x-y,\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nUse the trapezoidal method with two steps to approximate $y\\,(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1),$ rounded to two decimal places.\n\n$$\ny\\,(\\frac{1}{2})≈{{field-1}},\\,y(1)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9fce07b29e9bb957d3bba052038b804"} {"question" "q-339867" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe trapezoidal method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nFirst, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(1).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=1$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{1-0}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the trapezoidal method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{2}({y}^{′}+{y}_{\\text{new}}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}((x-y)+({x}_{\\text{new}}-{y}_{\\text{new}}))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}-y)-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y)-\\frac{1}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve for the general formula for ${y}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{4}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y) \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to calculate our approximations of $y(\\frac{1}{2})$ and $y(1).$\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nFrom the initial conditions, we have\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(0+\\frac{1}{2}+3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{10} \\\\  & =1.30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nFrom the previous step, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=1.30.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the new $x$ value is \n\n$$\n{x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the new $y$ value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+{x}_{\\text{new}}+3y) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(\\frac{1}{2}+1+3\\cdot (1.30)) \\\\  & =1.08.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ny(\\frac{1}{2})≈1.30,\\,y(1)≈1.08.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9fce07b29e9bb957d3bba052038b804"} {"question" "q-339867" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9446c68c60a0534a10a72f849a8cbe2e143d02ce1b73358e1b29ed25bed1591d"} {"question" "q-339867" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9fce07b29e9bb957d3bba052038b804"} {"question" "q-339867" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1.21"] ["math" "1.30"] ["math" "1.89"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14067188/state.json" "file_sha256" "98381986de5c63f4a92736416863e9c4d9f